## 3️⃣  What the code does, step‑by‑step

| Phase | What happens |
|-------|--------------|
| **Login** (`POST /login`) | After you verify the credentials, `setRememberMeCookie(res, user.id, { rememberMe })` creates a **signed** cookie named `remember_me`. The cookie contains the plain user‑id, is `httpOnly`, `secure` (when HTTPS), `SameSite=Strict`, and lives for **30 days** (`maxAge`). |
| **Subsequent request** (any route) | The `rememberMe` middleware runs early. It checks `req.signedCookies.remember_me`. If present, it extracts the user‑id, fetches the user from the DB (`db.getUserById`), and attaches the user object to `req.user`. If the user cannot be found, the stale cookie is cleared. |
| **Sliding expiration (optional)** | Inside the middleware we re‑`res.cookie` the same value with a fresh `maxAge`. This means every request pushes the expiry 30 days forward. Comment out that line if you prefer a fixed expiry from the moment the cookie was first created. |
| **Logout** (`POST /logout`) | `clearRememberMeCookie(res)` removes the cookie from the browser, effectively ending the “remember‑me” session. If you also use server‑side sessions, destroy them as well. |

