## 1️⃣ Table definition (PostgreSQL)

```sql
-- Run this once to create the table that stores the hashed keys
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS api_keys (
    id            SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_id   INTEGER NOT NULL,          -- or UUID, whatever you use for customers
    key_hash      TEXT    NOT NULL,          -- the hashed version of the API key
    created_at    TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE DEFAULT now(),
    revoked_at    TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE NULL,
    CONSTRAINT uq_customer_key UNIQUE (customer_id, key_hash)
);
```

> **Why store a hash?**  
> If the database is ever compromised the attacker only gets the hash, not the actual secret key that the client uses. The hash is created with a fast, cryptographically‑secure function (`sha256`) plus a per‑key random **salt**. The salt is stored together with the hash (concatenated) so we can recompute it later.

