## 3️⃣  Security & Gotchas

| Issue | Why it matters | How this middleware addresses it |
|-------|----------------|----------------------------------|
| **Wildcard `*` + credentials** | Browsers reject `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` when `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. | We always echo back the exact origin that was validated. |
| **Accidentally allowing a path or query string** | `Origin` headers never contain a path, but a mis‑configured whitelist entry could. | `normaliseOrigin` strips everything after the host (including path, query, fragment). |
| **Open‑redirect style attacks** | An attacker could try to inject `http://evil.com` via a malicious `Origin`. | Only origins present in the `originWhitelist` are ever echoed back. |
| **Stale pre‑flight cache** | If you change a partner’s allowed methods/headers, browsers may keep a cached pre‑flight response. | `Access-Control-Max-Age` defaults to 24 h; you can lower it (e.g. 600 s) if you need faster revocation. |
| **Large number of partners** | Each request does a Set lookup – O(1) – so even thousands of entries are cheap. | The whitelist is stored in a `Set`. |
| **CORS errors hidden in logs** | When an origin is not allowed we simply call `next()` without CORS headers; the browser reports the error. | This is intentional – you can add a custom logger if you want to record blocked attempts. |

