## 3️⃣  Why this works for **credentials** and **partner sites**

| Requirement | How the code satisfies it |
|-------------|---------------------------|
| **Credentials (cookies, Authorization header, etc.)** | `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` is always set when the origin is allowed. Because the spec forbids `*` when credentials are used, the middleware **echoes back the exact Origin header** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <origin>`). |
| **Different partner origins that change over time** | The whitelist lives in a `Set` that can be mutated at runtime via `cors.addOrigin()` / `cors.removeOrigin()`. No server restart is required. |
| **Pre‑flight (OPTIONS) handling** | The middleware intercepts `OPTIONS` requests, checks the origin, then sends `Access-Control-Allow-Methods`, `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` (either a static list or the request’s `Access-Control-Request-Headers`), `Access-Control-Max-Age` and ends with **204 No Content**. |
| **Simple requests** (GET, POST, etc.) | For non‑OPTIONS requests the same headers (`Allow-Origin`, `Allow-Credentials`, optional `Expose-Headers`) are added, then the request proceeds to your route handlers. |
| **Security** | Only whitelisted origins receive the CORS headers. All other origins get a **403** on pre‑flight and no CORS headers on normal requests, preventing browsers from exposing the response to the calling script. |

---

## 4️⃣  Quick checklist for production

| ✅ Item | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| **HTTPS** | CORS works the same over HTTP/HTTPS, but credentials (cookies, auth tokens) should only be sent over HTTPS. |
| **Origin validation** | Make sure you never add a wildcard (`*`) when `allowCredentials` is `true`. |
| **Header size limits** | Very long `Access-Control-Allow-Headers` values can hit server limits – keep the list reasonable. |
| **Cache** | `Access-Control-Max-Age` controls how long browsers cache the pre‑flight response. Adjust based on how often partner origins change. |
| **Logging / monitoring** | Consider logging rejected origins for audit purposes. |
| **Testing** | Use a tool like `curl -i -X OPTIONS -H "Origin: https://partner.com" -H "Access-Control-Request-Method: POST" http://localhost:3000/api/hello` to verify the pre‑flight response. |

