## 3. How it works (quick walkthrough)

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Origin check** | `req.headers.origin` is read. If it matches one of the whitelisted origins (or we fall back to `defaultOrigin`), `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` is set to that exact value. | The CORS spec forbids using `*` when `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` is `true`. Echoing the origin guarantees the browser accepts the response. |
| **Credentials** | `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` is always sent. | Required for cookie‑based auth, `Authorization` headers, or any other credentialed request from partners. |
| **Pre‑flight (OPTIONS)** | For `OPTIONS` requests we immediately respond with `204 No Content` and the appropriate `Allow‑*` headers (`Methods`, `Headers`, `Max‑Age`). | Browsers send a pre‑flight to verify what they may send. Responding quickly avoids hitting your actual route handlers. |
| **Normal request** | After setting the headers we call `next()` so your route logic runs as usual. | The API works unchanged; the only addition is the CORS headers. |
| **Dynamic partner management** | `corsHandler.addOrigin('https://new‑partner.com')` updates the in‑memory whitelist at runtime. | You can load partners from a DB, a config file, or an admin UI without restarting the server. |

