## 4. Extending / Persisting the whitelist

The example stores allowed origins in a plain `Set` in memory. In production you may want a persistent store (e.g., a DB table). The change is tiny:

```js
// pseudo‑code – replace the Set with a DB call
async function isOriginAllowed(requestOrigin) {
  const count = await db('allowed_origins')
    .where('origin', requestOrigin.toLowerCase())
    .count();
  return count > 0;
}
```

Just make `isOriginAllowed` async and adjust the middleware:

```js
async function middleware(req, res, next) {
  const requestOrigin = req.headers.origin;
  const allowed = await isOriginAllowed(requestOrigin);
  // …same logic as before…
}
```

Remember to wrap the async middleware with a small helper so Express can handle rejections:

```js
const asyncWrap = fn => (req, res, next) => Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
app.use(asyncWrap(corsHandler.middleware));
```

