## 3️⃣  Tips & Gotchas

| Issue | Why it matters | How to handle it |
|-------|----------------|------------------|
| **`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` + credentials** | Browsers reject the response if `*` is used together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`. | The middleware always **echoes the incoming origin** when credentials are enabled. |
| **Missing `Origin` header** | Some non‑browser clients (e.g., curl) won’t send it. | The middleware simply skips CORS processing for those requests. |
| **Dynamic origins that are patterns** | Partners may use sub‑domains (e.g., `*.partner.com`). | Pass a `RegExp` in the `origins` array: `/^https:\/\/.*\.partner\.com$/`. |
| **Large partner list** | If you have hundreds of partners, a Set lookup is O(1). | The current implementation already uses a `Set` plus optional RegExp checks. |
| **CORS errors still appear** | Make sure the middleware is **registered before any route** that sends a response. | `app.use(corsMw);` should be at the top of your middleware stack. |
| **Changing the whitelist at runtime** | The helper functions mutate the internal list; they **do not need a server restart**. | Call `cors.addOrigin('https://newpartner.com', corsMw);` whenever you onboard a new partner. |

